# The Missing Boundary Parameter in Multipart Uploads

When building a proxy or forwarding requests to an external API, it is easy to assume that setting `Content-Type: multipart/form-data` manually via `CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER` is sufficient. This approach fails because the HTTP protocol requires more than just the header name; it demands specific structural information embedded within the body itself.

In your scenario, you are reading a file into memory as raw binary data (`$imgRawData`) and attempting to send it directly. If you simply pass this raw string to `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS` while manually setting the Content-Type header, cURL will not generate the necessary boundary delimiters. The receiving API expects a specific format where every field is separated by a unique boundary string (e.g., `--Yt7gH5`). Without this, the server cannot distinguish where the image data ends and where other form fields begin.

To fix this, you must leverage cURL's ability to handle multipart constructs natively rather than constructing them manually. The standard library function `curl_file_create` was introduced specifically for this purpose. It takes a file path (or a stream resource) and automatically generates the correct boundary string and formats the body parts correctly.

Here is how you should restructure your PHP code:

```php
$ch = curl_init($url);

// Define the file to upload
$fileHandle = fopen('path/to/your/image.jpg', 'r'); // Or use a relative path from CLI context
$file = [
    'file' => curl_file_create($fileHandle, 'image/jpeg', 'original-image.jpg')
];

curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POST, 1);
// Pass the associative array to cURL; it handles multipart encoding automatically
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, $file); 

// Do NOT manually set Content-Type when using curl_file_create
// The library will calculate and set the boundary header automatically.

curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION, 1);
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_COOKIEFILE, $cookieJar);
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, 1);
curl_setopt( $ch, CURLOPT_HEADER, 1); // Only needed if you need to inspect raw headers

$response = curl_exec($ch);
$httpCode = curl_getinfo($ch, CURLINFO_HTTP_CODE);
curl_close($ch);

echo "Response: " . ($response ?: "Status Code: $httpCode");
```

There is a distinct trade-off between using `curl_file_create` and passing raw data directly. If you absolutely must pass raw binary data (for example, if the file handle is unavailable or coming from a different context), you can use an associative array where the value is simply the string data, but this requires careful handling to ensure the boundary logic works without automatic generation. However, in most cases involving image uploads, passing the local file path to `curl_file_create` is the robust solution.

The reason your previous attempt showed the correct `Content-Type` header in the debug dump yet failed on the API side is that cURL likely defaulted the Content-Type value to just `multipart/form-data` without including the boundary parameter (e.g., `multipart/form-data; boundary=----WebKitFormBoundary...`). Many servers, especially strict ones or older gateways, will reject requests missing this boundary parameter because they cannot parse the body structure.

By letting cURL manage the multipart encoding through an array of file objects, you ensure that the `boundary` token is appended to the header automatically. This aligns your request format exactly with what standard HTML forms do when submitted via a browser. If you are using Laravel for the backend handling this proxy logic, you can achieve similar results using the `UploadedFile` class, which wraps these raw cURL operations into a more expressive API.

Ultimately, manual manipulation of multipart bodies is fragile and prone to boundary mismatches. Trusting cURL's built-in multipart encoder via `curl_file_create` eliminates the complexity of calculating boundaries and ensures compatibility with any server expecting standard multipart/form-data uploads.
